"Take many samples of {#} from the same population by five methods and measure each method’s bias (does it center on the truth?) and spread (how far do its estimates scatter?). Then compare a self selected study with a randomized one and find the lurking variable that fools the first.",
"A <strong>population</strong> is everyone you want to know about; a <strong>sample</strong> is the part you actually measure. A <strong>parameter</strong> (the population mean μ) is a fixed number you usually do not know; a <strong>statistic</strong> (the sample mean x̄) is computed from the sample and changes from sample to sample. That change is <strong>sampling variability</strong>, and it is not a mistake: it is what any honest method gives.",
"The book’s methods (§{#}), for a sample of {#} from {#}:",
"<strong>Simple random sample:</strong> every group of {#} is equally likely to be chosen (names out of a hat, or a random number generator).",
"<strong>Stratified sample:</strong> split the population into groups that share something (strata: here the four sections of the hall) and take a simple random sample from each ({#} from each section).",
"<strong>Cluster sample:</strong> split the population into clusters (here {#} blocks of {#} neighboring seats), pick some clusters at random ({#} here) and take everyone in them.",
"<strong>Systematic sample:</strong> list everyone in order (seats {#} to {#}, row by row), pick a random start among the first {#}, then take every 20th.",
"<strong>Convenience sample:</strong> take whoever is easy to reach (here {#} of the {#} students nearest the door).",
"Two words describe a method, not a single sample. <strong>Bias</strong> is how far the method’s estimates are off on average: the mean of many x̄’s minus μ. <strong>Spread</strong> is how far its estimates scatter: the standard deviation s of many x̄’s. A good method has bias near zero and small spread. Taking more samples shows the bias more clearly, but never removes it.",
"How do you know whether a measured bias is real or just chance? The mean of {#} x̄’s has its own standard error, about s/√{#} = s/{#}. A measured bias several times bigger than s/{#} is real; one within about {#}(s/{#}) could be chance.",
"For a simple random sample of n from a population of N, the spread of x̄ is \\(\\sigma_{\\bar x} = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}\\sqrt{\\frac{N-n}{N-1}}\\), where σ is the population standard deviation; the last factor (the finite population correction) is {#} for n = {#} and N = {#}.",
"<strong>Experiments</strong> (§{#}). To test whether a treatment (watching review videos) changes a response (an exam score), you compare a group that got it with a group that did not. A <strong>lurking variable</strong> is something else that affects the response and also differs between the groups, so the difference you see is not all due to the treatment. When people choose their own group (<strong>self selection</strong>), whatever made them choose (here, how much they study) comes along. <strong>Random assignment</strong> makes the two groups alike on average in every variable, measured or not, so the only systematic difference left is the treatment.",
"The convenience sample takes students near the door. Give one reason students who sit near the door might differ from the rest of the class in how much change they carry (or in some other value), and say which way that would bias the estimate.",
